<Layout title="Lesson 13: Display Devices - C00lnerd Tutoring">
    <div style="max-width: 1000px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem;">
        
        <!-- Header -->
        <div style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%); color: white; padding: 3rem; border-radius: 15px; margin-bottom: 2rem; text-align: center;">
            <h1 style="font-size: 2.5rem; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 1rem 0;">Lesson 13: Display Devices</h1>
            <p style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 0 0 1rem 0; opacity: 0.9;">Put your robot arm's status on a real screen — no laptop required</p>
            <div style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.2); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 10px; display: inline-block;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: 600;">📺 128×64 pixels of pure status screen — angles, mode, and live joystick data</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Learning Objectives -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🎯 Learning Objectives</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem;">
                <div>
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">By the end of this lesson, you will:</h4>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>Wire a GME12864-13 OLED (SSD1306) using only 4 wires</li>
                        <li>Print text at pixel positions with <code>Adafruit_SSD1306</code></li>
                        <li>Show live servo angles, mode, and stick deflection on screen</li>
                        <li>Refresh a display using the draw-to-buffer / push-frame pattern</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Key Concepts:</h4>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>OLED vs LCD — pixels emit their own light</li>
                        <li>I2C bus — two shared wires, an address instead of pins</li>
                        <li>Framebuffer drawing — <code>display()</code> pushes the frame</li>
                        <li>Timed screen updates with <code>millis()</code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 1: Parts Inventory -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📦 Section 1: Parts Inventory</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem;">
                <div style="background: #e6fffa; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">New this lesson</h4>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>1 × <strong>GME12864-13</strong> — 0.96″ OLED, 128×64 px, SSD1306 driver, I2C (yellow top band, blue bottom)</li>
                        <li>4 × female-to-male jumper wires</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Carried over from Lesson 12</h4>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>Arduino Uno + USB cable</li>
                        <li>Joystick module + 2 SG90 servos</li>
                        <li>Breadboard and jumper wires</li>
                        <li>The whole working joystick control sketch</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #fef3c7; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #f59e0b;">
                <h4 style="color: #92400e; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">💡 OLED vs LCD</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    A character LCD has a backlight and stamps letters into fixed boxes. This OLED has <strong>no backlight at all</strong> — each of its 8,192 pixels is its own tiny light. That means two upgrades: you can draw <em>anywhere</em> on the screen (including graphics!), and black pixels are truly off.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Pin Map -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📌 Section 2: Pin Map &amp; Wiring</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                The GME12864-13 has just four pins: <strong>GND</strong>, <strong>VCC</strong>, <strong>SCL</strong> (clock), and <strong>SDA</strong> (data). On the Uno, the I2C bus lives on the last two analog pins — the same bus any I2C device can share:
            </p>

            <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Complete robot-arm pin map</h4>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.9;">
                    <li><strong>OLED SDA:</strong> A4 (I2C data)</li>
                    <li><strong>OLED SCL:</strong> A5 (I2C clock)</li>
                    <li><strong>OLED VCC / GND:</strong> power rail / ground rail (works on 3.3–5V)</li>
                    <li><strong>Joystick VRx / VRy / SW:</strong> A0 / A1 / A3 (unchanged)</li>
                    <li><strong>Button:</strong> A2, <strong>Buzzer (optional):</strong> pin 8</li>
                    <li><strong>Base servo:</strong> pin 9, <strong>Shoulder servo:</strong> pin 10</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #dc2626;">
                <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">⚠️ Check the silkscreen!</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    Not all OLED boards print the pins in the same order — most go <code>GND, VCC, SCL, SDA</code>, but some swap the first two or last two. <strong>Read the labels on YOUR board</strong> before wiring, not the cable colors.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Hello Display -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">👋 Section 3: Hello, Display</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Install two libraries: <strong>Sketch → Include Library → Manage Libraries</strong>, then install <code>Adafruit SSD1306</code> <em>and</em> <code>Adafruit GFX Library</code>. Now the classic first test:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>
#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>

#define SCREEN_WIDTH 128
#define SCREEN_HEIGHT 64
#define OLED_ADDR 0x3C   // most SSD1306 OLEDs; try 0x3D if blank

Adafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, -1);

void setup() {
    Serial.begin(9600);
    if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, OLED_ADDR)) {
        Serial.println("OLED not found - check wiring and address!");
        while (true);   // stop here - nothing else will work
    }

    display.clearDisplay();            // wipe the drawing buffer
    display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);
    display.setTextSize(2);            // 2x = ~12x16 px per char
    display.setCursor(0, 0);           // PIXEL position, not column/row!
    display.println("Hello,");
    display.println("arm!");
    display.setTextSize(1);            // ~6x8 px per char
    display.println();
    display.println("Base: ? Mode: ?");
    display.display();                 // push the buffer to the screen
}

void loop() {
    // nothing yet - the frame stays until the next display()
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #dc2626;">
                <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">⚠️ Black screen checklist</h4>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                    <li><strong>Serial says "not found"?</strong> Wrong address — change <code>0x3C</code> to <code>0x3D</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Still nothing?</strong> Check pin order on the board's silkscreen — SCL/SDA are the most commonly swapped pair.</li>
                    <li><strong>No contrast to adjust!</strong> Unlike LCDs there's no pot — if pixels light at all, it works.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Buffer concept -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🖼️ Section 4: The Framebuffer Pattern</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Here's the mind-bending part that makes OLEDs pleasant to code: <strong>nothing you draw appears until you call <code>display.display()</code></strong>. Every <code>print</code>, <code>fillRect</code>, and <code>drawCircle</code> writes into a hidden 1 KB buffer in the Arduino's RAM. Then one call ships the whole finished frame down the I2C wire.
            </p>

            <div style="background: #f8fafc; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <pre style="margin: 0; color: #4a5568; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.7; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;"><code>{`every refresh:
    clearDisplay()   ->  wipe the RAM buffer (screen unchanged!)
    draw everything  ->  text, numbers, shapes - still hidden
    display()        ->  whole frame appears at once, no flicker`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Compare with the character LCD world: there, <code>clear()</code> wiped the visible screen and caused flicker, so you had to overwrite fixed-width fields. On the OLED, "clearing" is free — it only erases <em>RAM</em>. The screen just jumps from old frame to new frame. And since pixels are addressed in <em>pixel coordinates</em>, <code>setCursor(64, 16)</code> means halfway across, 16 pixels down — not column 64, row 16.
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`unsigned long lastDisplayTime = 0;
const int displayInterval = 250;   // refresh 4x per second

// Print an angle right-aligned in a 3-char field
void printAngle(int n) {
    if (n < 100) display.print(" ");
    display.print(n);
}

void updateDisplay() {
    display.clearDisplay();                    // clear buffer only
    display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);

    // Title bar (lands in the yellow band)
    display.setTextSize(1);
    display.setCursor(0, 0);
    display.print("ARM STATUS   ");
    display.print(modeText);                   // "MANUAL"

    // Big angle readouts
    display.setTextSize(2);
    display.setCursor(0, 16);
    display.print("B:");
    printAngle(baseAngle);
    display.setCursor(64, 16);
    display.print("S:");
    printAngle(shoulderAngle);

    // Live stick deflection - debugging info
    display.setTextSize(1);
    display.setCursor(0, 48);
    display.print("dX:");
    display.print(lastDX);
    display.print("  dY:");
    display.print(lastDY);

    display.display();                         // push the frame
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Complete sketch -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🧩 Section 5: The Complete Sketch</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Lesson 12's joystick control, now reporting on the OLED. Count the timers again — <strong>motion every 15 ms, screen every 250 ms, button checked every pass</strong>. One chip, three jobs, zero <code>delay()</code>:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`// ============================================================
// Lesson 13: Display Devices (GME12864-13 OLED)
// Joystick-driven arm with live status on a 128x64 SSD1306.
//   X -> base (pin 9)   Y -> shoulder (pin 10)
//   SW click -> home   OLED -> angles + mode + deflection
// ============================================================

#include <Servo.h>
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>
#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>

Servo baseServo;
Servo shoulderServo;

#define SCREEN_WIDTH 128
#define SCREEN_HEIGHT 64
#define OLED_ADDR 0x3C
Adafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, -1);

// ---- Pins ----
const int joyX  = A0;
const int joyY  = A1;
const int joySW = A3;

// ---- Servo state ----
int baseAngle     = 90;
int shoulderAngle = 90;
const int MIN_ANGLE = 20;
const int MAX_ANGLE = 160;
const char modeText[] = "MANUAL";   // future: AUTO, etc.

// ---- Joystick calibration ----
int centerX = 512;
int centerY = 512;
const int deadZone = 25;
int lastDX = 0;                     // shown on the display
int lastDY = 0;

// ---- Independent timers ----
unsigned long lastStepTime    = 0;
unsigned long lastDisplayTime = 0;
const int stepInterval    = 15;    // servo update every 15 ms
const int displayInterval = 250;   // screen update every 250 ms
const int maxStep = 4;

int lastSWState = HIGH;

int signOf(int v) {
    return (v > 0) - (v < 0);
}

int stepFor(int deflection) {
    int d = abs(deflection);
    if (d < deadZone) return 0;
    return map(d, deadZone, 512, 1, maxStep);
}

void calibrateJoystick() {
    long sumX = 0, sumY = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 50; i++) {
        sumX += analogRead(joyX);
        sumY += analogRead(joyY);
        delay(10);
    }
    centerX = sumX / 50;
    centerY = sumY / 50;
}

void printAngle(int n) {           // right-align in 3 chars
    if (n < 100) display.print(" ");
    display.print(n);
}

void updateDisplay() {
    display.clearDisplay();
    display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);

    display.setTextSize(1);
    display.setCursor(0, 0);
    display.print("ARM STATUS   ");
    display.print(modeText);

    display.setTextSize(2);
    display.setCursor(0, 16);
    display.print("B:");
    printAngle(baseAngle);
    display.setCursor(64, 16);
    display.print("S:");
    printAngle(shoulderAngle);

    display.setTextSize(1);
    display.setCursor(0, 48);
    display.print("dX:");
    display.print(lastDX);
    display.print("  dY:");
    display.print(lastDY);

    display.display();
}

void setup() {
    Serial.begin(9600);
    baseServo.attach(9);
    shoulderServo.attach(10);
    pinMode(joySW, INPUT_PULLUP);
    baseServo.write(baseAngle);
    shoulderServo.write(shoulderAngle);

    if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, OLED_ADDR)) {
        Serial.println("OLED not found - check wiring/address");
        while (true);
    }
    display.clearDisplay();
    display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);
    display.setTextSize(1);
    display.setCursor(0, 20);
    display.println("Calibrating...");
    display.println("Hands off stick!");
    display.display();

    calibrateJoystick();
    updateDisplay();
}

void loop() {
    // Job 1: timed servo motion (every 15 ms)
    if (millis() - lastStepTime >= stepInterval) {
        lastStepTime = millis();
        lastDX = analogRead(joyX) - centerX;
        lastDY = analogRead(joyY) - centerY;
        baseAngle     = constrain(baseAngle     + signOf(lastDX) * stepFor(lastDX), MIN_ANGLE, MAX_ANGLE);
        shoulderAngle = constrain(shoulderAngle + signOf(lastDY) * stepFor(lastDY), MIN_ANGLE, MAX_ANGLE);
        baseServo.write(baseAngle);
        shoulderServo.write(shoulderAngle);
    }

    // Job 2: timed screen refresh (every 250 ms)
    if (millis() - lastDisplayTime >= displayInterval) {
        lastDisplayTime = millis();
        updateDisplay();
    }

    // Job 3: button check (every pass - no timer needed)
    int swState = digitalRead(joySW);
    if (swState == LOW && lastSWState == HIGH) {
        baseAngle = 90;
        shoulderAngle = 90;
    }
    lastSWState = swState;
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #ecfdf5; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #059669;">
                <h4 style="color: #065f46; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">🔍 Notice the layout math</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    The top ~16 pixels are the yellow band — that's why the title sits at y=0. Size-2 text is ~16 px tall, so angles sit at y=16. The debug line sits at y=48, comfortably in the blue zone. Pixel displays mean you design with a ruler, not rows.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Tuning & challenges -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🔧 Section 6: Tuning and Next Steps</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Text runs off the edge</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">At size 2 each character is ~12 px wide — only ~10 fit per line. Sketch the layout on paper first: x = columns of pixels, not character cells.</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Screen lags behind the arm</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">By design — 4 updates/sec. You can lower <code>displayInterval</code> to 100 safely; a full I2C frame just isn't free, so don't go every-loop.</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Display garbled when servos move</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">Servo current spikes corrupt I2C. Separate 5V supply for servos (shared GND!), keep SDA/SCL wires short.</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Sketch won't fit / crashes</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">The 1 KB framebuffer eats half the Uno's RAM. Avoid big String concatenation — use <code>print()</code> pieces like we do.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #0ea5e9;">
                <h4 style="color: #0c4a6e; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">🛠️ Put It Together — Challenges</h4>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.9;">
                    <li><strong>Joystick meter:</strong> draw a bar with <code>fillRect()</code> whose length follows <code>abs(lastDX)</code> — a live deflection gauge.</li>
                    <li><strong>Draw the arm:</strong> use <code>drawLine()</code> to sketch a simple two-segment arm matching the real angles. Trig required — that's the fun part.</li>
                    <li><strong>Add the elbow:</strong> 64 px tall fits four size-1 rows or two size-2 rows plus a footer — you don't need paging, just a new layout.</li>
                    <li><strong>Mode blink:</strong> make <code>modeText</code> flash every 500 ms when a "HOMING" mode is active — one more state variable, one more timer.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div style="text-center; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
                <a href="/programming/quizzes32/lesson13" style="background-color: #7c3aed; color: white; padding: 12px 24px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #6d28d9; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#6d28d9'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#7c3aed'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                    Take Lesson 13 Quiz →
                </a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Navigation -->
        <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; margin-top: 2rem;">
            <div>
                <a href="/programming/lessons32/lesson12" style="background-color: #6b7280; color: white; padding: 12px 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #4b5563; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#4b5563'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#6b7280'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                    ← Previous: Lesson 12
                </a>
